---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/drip-alternative';
const title = 'Best Drip Alternatives for Shopify (2026)';
const desc = 'Compare Drip alternatives through workflow ownership and event mapping, practical workflow checks and matched operating costs.';
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Why consider leaving Drip?",
    "answer": "A documented maintenance, integration or operating-cost gap can justify review. First assign an owner and reproduce the problem. A handover failure is not evidence that Drip cannot support the workflow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does Drip require a separate SMS vendor?",
    "answer": "Not necessarily. Drip advertises staged integrated SMS access for existing paid US customers with Support All People enabled. Phone records require email; launch scope is one-way toll-free sending without SMS-only subscribers, keyword joining, short codes or two-way replies. Verify access before changing an existing SMS arrangement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Sequenzy automatically easier or cheaper?",
    "answer": "No. Sequenzy documents AI drafting, a visual builder and send-volume billing with unlimited contacts. Compare rebuilding and editing the same workflow, plus matched all-in costs. Draft generation does not demonstrate correct Shopify event mapping or purchase suppression."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should a Drip workflow specification contain?",
    "answer": "Record the trigger and event payload, tag and custom-field definitions, delay, branches, goal and exit rules, current audience, queue state, integrations and owner. Prioritize economically important journeys while retaining explanations for subscription, gift and group-buy exclusions."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can the old and new platforms coexist?",
    "answer": "Yes, with a written division of journeys. Assign one sender and queue owner to each journey. Preserve permission evidence by purpose and channel, withdrawals, suppressions and current purchase state. Stop old entry, drain or cancel pending messages, deduplicate replayed events and test rollback before enabling replacement sends. A fixed overlap period cannot establish readiness."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should I compare migration cost?",
    "answer": "Compare the same contacts, sends, channels and required workflows. Include platform fees, add-ons, overages, implementation, support, maintenance and overlapping subscriptions. Separate attributed orders from incremental contribution after discounts and returns; one recovered cart does not establish payback."
  },
  {
    "question": "What should transfer with subscribers?",
    "answer": "Export field definitions, permission sources and timestamps, withdrawal history, suppressions, journey membership and relevant order state. Verify the actual export and import fields. Quarantine records whose eligibility is unresolved; an importable email address is not evidence of permission for every purpose or channel."
  },
  {
    "question": "When can the old account be closed?",
    "answer": "After event mapping, current eligibility checks, purchase exits, queue reconciliation, sender authentication and rollback have passed. Export needed documentation under applicable access and retention terms. Choose an observation period appropriate to the product buying cycle rather than imposing a universal deadline."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<div class="container py-14 max-w-4xl">
<nav><a class="link" href="/alternatives">Alternatives</a></nav>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
<TldrBox title="Decision checks" picks={[
{ label: 'Workflow', tool: 'Current setup first', reason: 'Reproduce the unmet requirement before selecting a replacement.' },
{ label: 'Migration', tool: 'One queue owner', reason: 'Preserve eligibility, purchase exits and replay deduplication.' },
{ label: 'Economics', tool: 'Matched workload', reason: 'Compare actual invoices and quotes; measure contribution separately.' },
]} />
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Start with the workflow that needs to change</h2><p>Replacing Drip starts with recovering workflow knowledge. A replacement must preserve the meaning of tags, purchase events, delays and exits before it can improve maintenance or cost. For a keyboard shop, group-buy exclusions are a more useful acceptance case than the number of nodes in a builder.</p><p>This guide proposes acceptance exercises, not performed merchant tests. Supplier documentation establishes advertised scope; it does not establish launch speed, comparative performance, payback or legal permission for a particular recipient. Alternatives appear as a shortlist, without a score or universal winner.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Documented scope before comparison</h2><p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging">Shopify Messaging documentation</a> covers native campaigns and automations. Its <a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/marketing-automations">automation guide</a> distinguishes channel eligibility and directs custom or third-party workflows to Shopify Flow. Default marketing subscription checks and configurable checkout behavior need account review, not assumptions about universal permission.</p><p><a class="link" href="https://www.tinyemail.com/faqs">tinyEmail’s current FAQ</a> advertises Shopify connectivity, automation, unlimited contacts and a Shopify-specific free send allowance. Check eligibility and add-ons rather than carrying forward an old contact-tier estimate or a campaign-only label.</p><p><a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/features/shopify-integration">Sequenzy’s Shopify page</a> documents customer, order and product sync and cart, post-purchase and winback sequences. Its <a class="link" href="https://www.sequenzy.com/pricing">pricing page</a> describes send-volume billing, unlimited contacts, AI drafting and a visual builder. Test custom consumption data, gift and subscription exclusions; these documents do not prove that a natural-language request produces a validated merchant policy.</p><p><a class="link" href="https://www.drip.com/sms">Drip’s SMS page</a> describes staged access for existing paid US customers with Support All People enabled. It requires an email with a phone record and lists one-way toll-free sending at launch. Check actual access, number arrangements and separate SMS charges before replacing or retaining an SMS specialist.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>When preserving Drip is the better change</h2><p>Keep the existing branches when they express a policy the store still needs and the failure is missing ownership. For the tea shop, a documented blend tag, subscription exclusion and purchase exit can be worth preserving. Assign a backup, remove obsolete paths and measure the effort of one real policy edit before commissioning a rebuild. A replacement builder does not supply the missing policy owner.</p><p>Preserve measurement meaning too. Write down which order event starts attribution, its window, how refunds and discounts enter the report, and whether a workflow total includes repeat buyers who would have purchased anyway. Keep a dated Drip baseline and reconcile order identifiers with store records. A new dashboard using a different window can change reported revenue without changing customer behavior. If the only promised benefit is a larger attributed total, there is no demonstrated reason to move.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Choose a replacement for a specific missing job</h2><p>The following are proposed evaluation briefs. Ask each candidate to demonstrate the relevant requirement with the Drip branch specification; these are not scored product tests. Request plan and integration confirmation before relying on a feature or service.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Sequenzy</h2><p>An AI-assisted rebuild of a small, understood lifecycle library is a reason to evaluate Sequenzy. Its documented sync and visual builder give the operator a concrete starting point: draft the keyboard welcome path, then compare every group-buy exception with the Drip specification. Have the backup operator change one exclusion and explain the resulting audience.</p><p>The useful benefit to establish is less drafting and editing work. The limit is policy translation: AI copy and a drawn branch do not establish equivalent event semantics. Quote quiet and peak send volumes, then add the human review and rebuild work before comparing with the current Drip invoice.</p><p><a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy and Drip comparison</a></p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Klaviyo</h2><p>Make Klaviyo the data-requirement candidate when the outdoor store has a specific unresolved variant-restock or collection-interest rule. Bring the actual payload and ask where variant identity, inventory state and interest history will live, which integration updates each field, and what happens when the update arrives late.</p><p>A richer data model is useful only if it answers that missing question. Ask who maintains the connector and who can edit the segment after handover. Quote the required profiles, events, sends and channels; do not fund the rebuild on an assumed data ceiling in Drip or an assumed revenue uplift.</p><p><a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip">Klaviyo and Drip comparison</a></p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Omnisend</h2><p>Evaluate Omnisend for a deliberate consolidation of email and SMS operations. Use one cart that progresses to checkout and then purchase; ask the vendor to show the chosen journey templates, cross-channel stop conditions and where channel-specific permission is checked. Compare those steps with the current Drip plus SMS-vendor arrangement.</p><p>The potential benefit is fewer separately maintained journey configurations. The contract question is whether the offered plan, destinations, number arrangements and SMS charges cover the required operation. Compare this with actual Drip SMS access described above; neither shared branding nor a shared profile proves that both channels can send to the same person.</p><p><a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-drip">Omnisend and Drip comparison</a></p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Sendlane</h2><p>Treat Sendlane as a managed-help evaluation when the Drip operator has left and the team cannot safely rebuild alone. Send the workflow inventory before requesting an onboarding proposal. Ask for named deliverables: tag definitions recovered, branches rebuilt, templates transferred, purchase exits exercised and a backup operator trained.</p><p>Guidance has value only with an agreed boundary. Identify who resolves a subscription-app mapping error after launch, which corrections are included and when support becomes separately billable. Compare the written service scope with repairing Drip using paid help; do not infer a guaranteed migration service or completion date from a sales conversation.</p><p><a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-sendlane">Sendlane and Drip comparison</a></p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>ActiveCampaign</h2><p>For a pottery business mixing wholesale inquiries with DTC purchases, evaluate ActiveCampaign against the sales handoff job. Trace a shop buyer requesting a trade account: who owns follow-up, which status should stop retail offers, and which system writes that status back to the marketing record? Ask for a demonstration on the proposed plan and connectors.</p><p>The benefit to establish is a reliable handoff between sales and marketing. The limit is another source of state to maintain. A wholesale status must not silently replace Shopify purchase or subscription state. If the store has no sales pipeline requirement, rebuilding the Drip library around one adds work without solving the stated problem.</p><p><a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign">ActiveCampaign and Drip comparison</a></p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Brevo</h2><p>Evaluate Brevo as a cost-reduction candidate only after deciding which Drip branches the store will actually retain. Give it the vinyl shop’s quiet-month and release-week send schedules, including bursts, transactional traffic kept elsewhere and the required automation audience. Request the exact plan and connector costs for that workload.</p><p>The potential benefit is a better matched bill for a simpler retained program. The acceptance limit is the surviving branch list: demonstrate late purchases, catalog fields and exclusions before approving any reduction in workflow scope. Compare the quote with a pruned Drip account; deleting necessary journeys to make a quote smaller is a different business decision.</p><p><a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-brevo">Brevo and Drip comparison</a></p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Mailchimp</h2><p>Consider Mailchimp when the team already operates there for another brand and wants shared training and campaign production. Ask that operator to reproduce one retained Drip journey using the actual tags and purchase events, then edit a template and exclusion without help from the departing builder.</p><p>Existing familiarity may reduce training, but templates, permissions and journey state still need a transfer plan. Obtain a quote using the candidate’s billable audience definition and required automation access. Choose it only if the retained jobs pass; neither an automatic capability downgrade nor an automatic saving follows from the product name.</p><p><a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp">Mailchimp and Drip comparison</a></p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Shopify Messaging</h2><p>Use Shopify Messaging for a deliberate reduction to store-owned campaigns and required native automations. Map each retained Drip journey to a native automation, a specified Flow extension or an explicit retirement decision. For a simple tea catalog, staying in the store admin may be a lasting operating choice.</p><p>The benefit to establish is fewer external mappings and accounts. The limit is every exception that still needs custom state: assign ownership for blend preferences and subscription updates rather than dropping them in the reset. Include the documented allowance and automation counting rules below, plus any Flow or app work, in the native cost baseline.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Pair-specific trial scenarios</h2></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Keyboard shop: owner handover</h2><p>Proposed exercise: inventory eight workflows and forty nodes, then ask a backup operator to explain group-buy exclusions. Rebuild one path from the specification, replay an event and purchase during its delay. Record editing and review effort; no maintenance saving or preserved revenue is claimed.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Outdoor gear: 35,000-contact quote workload</h2><p>Use shell-layer and harness variants to test restock eligibility and winback conditions against actual event payloads. Quote both active and inactive records under each provider’s billable definition. Contact count alone establishes neither a data ceiling nor a cost winner.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Specialty tea: retain Drip</h2><p>A small catalog may remain on Drip with a named workflow owner. Audit blend-preference tags, subscription data and replenishment exclusions. Use an illustrative 8,000 engaged contacts to compare invoices and maintenance work; staying can pass the same criteria as migration.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Furniture: discount restraint</h2><p>For an illustrative $840 basket, compare a ninety-minute reminder with a three-hour delay and financing FAQ instead of an automatic ten-percent coupon. Verify purchase and permission suppression first. A suitable holdout can estimate incremental margin; no lift or rollout duration has been observed.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Vinyl: list growth and billing</h2><p>For an illustrative 28,000-contact list, distinguish captured addresses, eligible recipients and actual monthly sends. Include support, extra channels and overlap in quotes. Review inactive records before retention or deletion; there is no universal profile-count break-even.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Migration checklist and queue ownership</h2><p>The original migration jobs remain relevant. Adapt their order to dependencies and risk; a fixed launch calendar cannot replace readiness checks.</p></section>
<ol>
          <li><strong>Export subscribers with tags and custom fields</strong> — include field definitions, not just column headers.</li>
          <li><strong>Screenshot every active workflow</strong> — triggers, delays, branches, exit goals, Shopify events used.</li>
          <li><strong>Identify top three revenue workflows</strong> — rebuild these first on new platform; retire dead experiments.</li>
          <li><strong>Sunset inactive profiles</strong> — inspect engagement, deliverability and retention requirements before deleting or suppressing records; no universal inactive percentage applies.</li>
          <li><strong>Map integrations</strong> — subscription apps, loyalty, reviews pushing data into Drip tags.</li>
          <li><strong>Connect replacement to Shopify</strong> — verify event sync before contact import.</li>
          <li><strong>Rebuild welcome and cart</strong> — use test orders to check current permission, unsubscribe and purchase state at send time. Assign one sender per journey, drain old queues and deduplicate replayed events before enabling replacement sends.</li>
          <li><strong>Validate suppression through cutover</strong> — cover the longest active delay and retry window; elapsed days alone do not prove old queued abandonment sends have stopped.</li>
          <li><strong>Migrate winback and replenishment</strong> — tea and coffee windows are time-sensitive.</li>
          <li><strong>Repoint capture tools</strong> — Privy, Justuno integrations feeding new ESP.</li>
          <li><strong>Warm sending domain if infrastructure changes</strong> — see <a class="link" href="/use-cases/deliverability">deliverability guide</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Plan reference retention</strong> — export the needed workflow documentation before cancellation, subject to access terms and data retention requirements.</li>
        </ol><section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Translate branch meaning before rebuilding the canvas</h2><p>For each Drip branch, write the question it answers in merchant language: purchased this variant, holds an unfulfilled group buy, subscribes to this blend, or has already received the offer. Then identify the event or field that answers it. A tag named “customer” may describe a past import rather than current purchase state. Moving that tag without its update rule preserves the label and loses the decision.</p><p>Build a mapping sheet with source event, customer identifier, order identifier, timestamp, field type and updating integration. Include one missing value and one late update in each important branch exercise. For a keyboard preorder, replay a purchase after the reminder is scheduled and check that a later fulfillment event cannot restart the sales path. This is a proposed acceptance test, not a claim about either provider’s behavior.</p><p>Capture tools and subscription apps belong in that sheet. Repoint one input only after confirming how it creates or updates a person and how withdrawals reach the replacement. Keep order confirmations with their notification owner while rebuilding promotional paths; a marketing migration need not move every message.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Matched billing worksheet</h2><p>Build two quote workloads: a quiet period and a representative peak. Record retained contacts, billable contacts under each supplier’s definition, total sends, capture traffic, destinations and required features. Add SMS segments, extra apps, support, overages, migration labor and overlapping subscriptions.</p><p><a class="link" href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/promoting-marketing/create-marketing/shopify-messaging/email/pricing/">Shopify email pricing</a> documents 10,000 monthly free emails on supported plans, with ordinary automations counting and abandoned-checkout emails excluded. Treat the native allowance and tinyEmail’s Shopify offer as real comparison inputs rather than assuming every captured profile adds cost.</p><p>Use current written quotes for all other suppliers. A price indexed only by contact count cannot establish a send-volume platform’s total. Avoid numeric automation ratings; attach a pass, failure or unresolved requirement to each tested workflow instead.</p></section>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Cost line</th><th>Current setup</th><th>Candidate</th><th>Evidence needed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Platform and channels</td><td>Actual invoice</td><td>Matched quote</td><td>Billing definition, sends and destinations</td></tr><tr><td>Implementation</td><td>Maintenance baseline</td><td>Rebuild and review</td><td>Named tasks and recorded operator hours</td></tr><tr><td>Overlap</td><td>Old queues and access</td><td>Activation and rollback</td><td>One sender per journey</td></tr><tr><td>Contribution</td><td>Comparable baseline</td><td>Incremental estimate</td><td>Discounts, returns and uncertainty</td></tr></tbody></table><section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Price the workflow library and account exit</h2><p>Count active branches and external dependencies alongside messages. A small list with subscription exceptions can take more review work than a larger list running one newsletter. Request separate estimates for field mapping, workflow rebuilds, template transfer, authentication, operator training and rollback. Compare that one-time effort with fixing the documented maintenance problem inside Drip.</p><p>Assign each delayed journey a cutover decision: drain it in Drip, cancel and restart only eligible people, or leave that journey there temporarily. Do not import everyone at the beginning of a sequence merely because their contact record transfers. Record which queued step each person would receive next and how recent purchases and withdrawals will be reconciled.</p><p>Before closing the account, export the workflow definitions and reports needed to explain historical decisions, confirm access and retention terms, and remove obsolete capture connections. A backup operator should be able to change the group-buy exclusion from the mapping sheet. If that handover still depends on the original builder’s memory, the maintenance problem has survived the migration.</p></section>
<section class="prose-shopify mt-12"><h2>Related guides</h2><p><a class="link" href="/apps/drip">Drip review</a> · <a class="link" href="/alternatives">All alternatives</a> · <a class="link" href="/use-cases/deliverability">Deliverability planning</a></p></section>

<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip alternatives FAQ" />
</div>
</BaseLayout>
